-- GTC-325 — the press writes a row for the people it decided NOT to message.
--
-- ⚠ ONE STATEMENT, ZERO DATA. Generated by `prisma migrate dev --create-only
-- --skip-generate` and authored into ABOVE the generated line; the generated SQL below is
-- untouched, which is the distinction Zone 5 draws and the precedent
-- `20260918062839_gtc312_backfill_phone_number` set.
--
-- WHY THE COLUMN LOSES ITS NOT NULL. Founder ruling, 2026-09-19, shape 3: `OutboundMessage`
-- stops meaning ONE ROW PER ADDRESSED RECIPIENT and starts meaning ONE ROW PER PERSON THE
-- PRESS DECIDED ABOUT — "the outbound row is the record of what the press decided about a
-- person, and deciding not to message someone is a decision about them." A person on the
-- host's list has NO channel; that is precisely why they are on the list. Three ways out
-- were offered and the founder took this one:
--
--   "Nullable. Not a NONE member, and certainly not EMAIL by convention, which you are
--    right to refuse: a column saying EMAIL about a person with no channel is the
--    false-sentence family, and this ledger has caught it four times. NONE is the tempting
--    middle and it is wrong for a quieter reason — it makes 'no channel' a kind of channel,
--    and the next reader has to learn that one of the three members is not one."
--
-- WHAT IT UNBLOCKS. Slice 7a reads the delivery fact OFF THE ROW, so ruling M's UNREACHABLE
-- red has never fired for anybody: the press wrote no row for a host-list person, and four
-- people on the one pressable board read AMBER about a decision Gather had already made.
-- After this, 7a's mechanism sees them with no edit at all — `NO_CHANNEL` and its four
-- siblings already map to UNREACHABLE, waiting for a row that carries them.
--
-- ⚠ AND IT IS A PRECONDITION OF [[GTC-322]], WHICH WAS RULED FIRST AND RE-ORDERED BEHIND IT
-- on 2026-09-19. That backfill writes a row for a person no chooser ever routed, and the
-- only honest channel for such a row is this NULL.
--
-- SAFETY, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED — see the ticket's rehearsal evidence:
--   * `ALTER COLUMN ... DROP NOT NULL` is a catalogue update in Postgres. It rewrites no
--     rows and takes no data lock beyond ACCESS EXCLUSIVE for the duration of the catalogue
--     write. Proven on a clone by reading `pg_class.relfilenode` before and after: unchanged.
--   * `OutboundMessage` holds 0 rows in `gather_dev` today, so there is nothing to rewrite
--     even if it did. That makes the rehearsal quiet, which was a prediction to verify
--     rather than a reason to skip it.
--   * IT IS WIDENING, SO IT IS BACKWARD-COMPATIBLE WITH EVERY EXISTING ROW: every row that
--     satisfied NOT NULL still satisfies a nullable column. Nothing existing becomes invalid.
--   * ⚠ AND IT IS NOT TRIVIALLY REVERSIBLE ONCE NULLS EXIST. Restoring NOT NULL would fail
--     while any host-list row is present, which is by design — the rows are the point.
--
-- WHAT THIS MIGRATION DOES NOT DO: it writes no row, changes no reader, and by itself changes
-- nothing a host sees. `pressSend` writing the host-list rows ships in the same commit as
-- this file; the `UNREACHABLE` red starts firing at the first press AFTER both.

-- AlterTable
ALTER TABLE "OutboundMessage" ALTER COLUMN "channel" DROP NOT NULL;
